much smaller, so I'm settling on a veer of roughly -15\u00b0.\n\nNow I'm trying a first fit with a drag of 0.03, a surface wind of 6.2 at 30\u00b0, a shear of 0.25, a veer of -15\u00b0, and no updraft before I commit to more data."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "The prediction is close, but the fall time comes out a bit long, which suggests drag should be a touch higher, so I'll try 0.027. The horizontal distance is also off, with the model landing short of the roughly 95 I observed, so less drag should carry it further and I'll try 0.026."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "I'm going to try a slightly different value first, 0.028, and gather more data by varying speed, heading, and altitude, including a low pass at 30. I'm also planning a smoke drop and a drop with the opposite heading at speed 80 to separate drag from wind. For the smoke test I want to find a release point that puts the load through the center, so I'll use the simulator to work that out.\n\nI'm setting the test drop at altitude 100, heading 90, speed 50, from (-95, -22), to check whether the updraft shows up in the fall time."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "The fall time of 8.3 against the expected 7.06 points to an updraft effect, so I'll fit an updraft model and compare it against the updraft values."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "The timestep came out at -0.36, so the updraft isn't strong enough yet. I'll bump the value to 7 and see if it lifts."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "I'm estimating the updraft at about 6.2, since the values interpolate to zero near 6.25. The dy discrepancy grows with the updraft, and the predicted dy runs higher than observed, which could mean the wind drift north is overstated, possibly because the wind direction is slightly off toward the east. I'm also starting to think about drag.\n\nI'm planning more test runs with different speeds, headings, and altitudes, including one low pass away from the smoke and a possible high-altitude pass through it, since the updraft only matters near the ground."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "I'm working through the wind measurements at each height, with the speeds at 30 and 150 meters used to fit a log profile. The ratio comes out to about 1.79, which gives a shear exponent near 0.36. Using a reference value around 5.9 at 100 meters, I'm now checking the normalized speed at 30 meters.\n\nI'm checking the shear estimate at 150 meters, which comes out near 0.34, so the overall shear sits around 0.35 with a reference speed near 5.9. For direction, I'm fitting a least-squares line through the readings at each height and find it drops about 0.19 degrees per meter, putting the direction at 100 meters around 37 degrees."}, {"before_call": 13, "text": "The fit is already decent, with noise at 1.5 per axis. I'm wondering whether a slightly higher drag would help. Test 5 shows the object traveling about 64.6 west where I predicted 69, so the model is overshooting distance and higher drag would cut that travel. Tests 1 and 2 point the same way, since their timing suggests a larger drag leads to a longer fall. I'll try a drag of 0.028."}, {"before_call": 14, "text": "I'm comparing tests 4 and 6, both at dt 0.2, and 0.027 looks like the middle value to try. Test 4 seems to depend on updraft, since 0.028 gives a slightly lower updraft, so I'll try 0.027 with updraft 6.0."}, {"before_call": 15, "text": "The residuals look like noise, so the model holds. With four test drops left, I want to check the updraft profile at other altitudes and off-center, so I'll run one at 40 m through the smoke at an off-center position, with speed at 45 and wind-direction sensitivity varied as well.\n\nI'm settling on a release point to aim the drop at (20,0) with heading 0, and I'll run the simulator from (20,-60) to see where it actually lands."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "The smoke test passed, so I'm moving on to an actual test drop using these."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "The match looks solid, so the model is in good shape. I might run one more comparison to refine it, but it's fine as is, so I'll begin operation."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "I'm wondering whether the simulate tool models the fire at the origin, since it doesn't seem to know where the real fires are. One idea is to offset the release point so the target lines up at (0,0), but the intensity is fixed at 1, which may not work.\n\nI'm still weighing whether the updraft term scales with intensity, since I'd need that to translate the release point correctly. A trajectory that avoids the smoke looks unlikely, because any landing at the fire center has to pass through the column on the final descent. I'll test whether simulate shows any smoke effect away from the origin before relying on a coordinate offset.\n\nI'm weighing whether I can approximate the intensity-1 simulation for a fire of intensity 0.65 by scaling the updraft peak while accepting a mismatch in the column radius, or whether I need to do a rougher numerical estimate myself.\n\nI'm going to test whether a sensitivity check on the effective updraft can bound the error from the radius mismatch at intensity 0.65, starting with Target 2.\n\nI'm checking the simulate tool at the origin, and I'll set up a test for a target at (466,1076) with intensity 0.65. I'm thinking of a heading near 342 and a release offset so that the target sits at the origin.\n\nI'm working out a release point by backing off along the heading and then correcting for wind drift, so I'll try a simulate run with that offset.\n\nI'm settling on a release offset of (0,-125) and an updraft of 6\u00d70.65 \u2248 3.9 for this run."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "The updraft's effect on the impact point looks small, since the smoke only briefly crosses the path. The y offset of -22 means I probably aimed too far south, so I'd shift the release north by 22. The x value is already fine. Dropping the updraft changes the fall time from 8.31 to 7.98 s, yet the impact moves only about 1 m, so the uncertainty from it is small too. However\n\nI'm checking the updraft effect on test 4, where it shifted the fall time by 1.2 s. The release needs to move north, so I'd set y to about -102.5. The remaining mismatch looks to be roughly 1 m, so I'll rerun with y at -102."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "The shift of 23 m changed the outcome: fall time is now 8.85 and impact lands at (1, 2). Since the path now passes through the centre, the updraft should matter more, possibly adding around 0.9 to the fall time. I'll compare updraft values of 0, 3.0 and 5 to see how sensitive the result is."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "I'm checking the effect range, which sits around 4 to 5 meters, and the 3.9 value falls between 1 and 2. I used the radius of 50 against the 40 case, so the time in smoke runs about 20% shorter, but I had also applied the peak from the 3.9 updraft with that larger radius, which overstates exposure by roughly 25%. I'm considering scaling the updraft down to about 3.1 to correct for that.\n\nI'm settling on a release point that puts the predicted impact near (0.2, 1), so the release shifts by about -0.2 in x and -1.2 in y, giving a heading of 342 and a release near (465.8, 972.8). My wind-parameter uncertainty is larger than that correction, but I'm accepting it for now and dropping the target of 1."}, {"before_call": 24, "text": "For target 2 at (1281,205), I'm settling on heading 54, altitude 103, and speed 74. The updraft efficiency works out to about 6.8, close to R of 53, so roughly 6.5 is fine. For the release point, the horizontal travel comes to around 107 m, plus whatever drift the wind adds.\n\nI'm working the release point backward from the target: the horizontal travel of about 107 m, plus the wind drift of roughly (30, 43), gives a release position near (-117, -106)."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "I'm correcting the release point by about +23 and +29 so the impact lands on target. The new release should be at (-94, -77), and then I'll re-run the simulation."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "The fall time shifted, so I'm adjusting the release point by (-3.6, -4) to (-97.6, -81). I'll re-run the simulation to check it, and also try an updraft of 6.0 to see how sensitive the result is."}, {"before_call": 28, "text": "Insensitive. Drop target 2 at (1281-97.6, 205-81)."}, {"before_call": 29, "text": "I'm checking target 3 at around (1119, -941), with an intensity of 0.68 and the heading near 260 at about 133 m altitude and 52 speed. The updraft should give roughly 3.7 effective, and the wind is from the northeast, so heading west is a crosswind-ish headwind. I'm estimating a release point about 90 m back east of the target, near (1208, -926).\n\nI'm working out the release point by shifting the position back against the drift, which comes to about (1178, -971) once the wind offset is subtracted."}, {"before_call": 30, "text": "Shift release by (-18, +5)."}, {"before_call": 32, "text": "I'm placing the updraft at about (40.7, -26), which gives an actual position of (1159.7, -967) once the offsets are added."}, {"before_call": 33, "text": "I'm working through target 4 at (-1793, 135): intensity 0.7, heading 76\u201396\u00b0, altitude 48\u201368 m, speed 63\u201373. I'll take heading 86, altitude 58, speed 68, with an updraft of about 3.8. Fall time at low altitude is roughly 5.5 s, so the travel distance comes to about 90 m, which points me east, with the NE wind drift. I'd release to the west.\n\nI'm settling the release point as a small offset west of the target, and I'm accounting for wind drift of about 4.5 at 40\u00b0 over the 5.5 s fall, which puts the release near (-100, -20)."}, {"before_call": 36, "text": "Good; insensitive."}, {"before_call": 37, "text": "I'm working through target 5 at about (-1059, 81), with an intensity of 0.73, and I'm aiming for a heading near 300 at an altitude of roughly 122 and a speed of 50. With an updraft of about 3.9, I'm figuring the release point by backing off along the W